    14	# Design analysis for combo D6/0 + D12/0
    15	# ---------------------------------------
    16	# a) Concrete behavior and trace
    17	#   - Baseline abc82100 learns local legends of the form:
    18	#       source pixel -> target pixel -> nearby teal stencil.
    19	#     Every scattered source pixel of that color receives a translated copy of the
    20	#     teal stencil recolored uniformly to the target color.
    21	#   - D6/0 changes the copied object: the template now has a teal backbone plus
    22	#     1-3 fixed accent cells. When copied, teal cells become the rule's target
    23	#     color, but accent cells keep their own colors.
    24	#   - D12/0 changes overlap semantics from "later write overwrites earlier write"
    25	#     to a genuine order-free merge. For this combo, we make the OR semantics
    26	#     visible and inferable by splitting template cells into two roles:
    27	#       * recolored teal cells = mutable layer
    28	#       * non-teal accent cells = fixed layer
    29	#     If multiple stamps touch the same output cell, any fixed-layer claim wins
    30	#     over mutable-layer claims; within one layer we only generate same-color
    31	#     overlaps. Example:
    32	#       rule A legend shows red->yellow next to a teal zig-zag with one blue
    33	#       accent. rule B legend shows green->orange next to another template. If a
    34	#       scattered A stamp places its blue accent on a cell that a scattered B
    35	#       stamp also paints orange via teal, the output keeps blue there. The union
    36	#       is therefore order-independent and visibly different from overwrite.
    37	#
    38	# b) Complexity calibration
    39	#   - Every rule has at least one accent cell, so the task cannot silently reduce
    40	#     to the monochrome original.
    41	#   - Each sample is forced to contain at least one accent-over-target overlap, so
    42	#     OR merging is not merely theoretical.
    43	#   - Accent count stays in 1-3 and teal stencil size stays small, keeping the
    44	#     legends readable while still making overlap behavior visually obvious.
    45	#
    46	# c) Hidden assumptions broken by the combo
    47	#   - Original rule discovery stored only teal component coordinates; that loses
    48	#     accent cells, so the solver must extract the full multicolor template.
    49	#   - Original preservation logic kept every non-bg non-teal pixel outside legend
    50	#     pairs. Under D6 this would wrongly preserve legend accent cells in output.
    51	#   - Original stamping line `out[...] = target_color` encoded overwrite order.
    52	#     Under D12/0 the solver must accumulate claims first and resolve them after
    53	#     all stamps have been considered.
    54	#
    55	# d) Placement legality
    56	#   - Legends keep a Chebyshev-1 halo around their whole template region, so all
    57	#     cells within distance 1 of a teal component belong only to that template.
    58	#   - Scattered source pixels stay pairwise non-4-adjacent and outside legend
    59	#     halos, preventing accidental fake legends.
    60	#   - Overlaps are constrained to the intended family: fixed-vs-target or same-
    61	#     color same-layer. The generator forbids fixed-vs-fixed color conflicts and
    62	#     forbids different target colors on the same cell.
    63	#
    64	# e) Visual observability
    65	#   - Humans can read the legend as "teal changes, accent stays". The output then
    66	#     shows that when translated copies collide, the fixed accent survives.
    67	#   - Because every sample includes at least one mixed overlap and also several
    68	#     ordinary non-overlapping placements, the difference from both the original
    69	#     task and pure D6 is visible from a few examples.
    70	#
    71	# f) Visual cleanliness
    72	#   - Legend regions are separated by halos; only output stamps are allowed to
    73	#     overlap.
    74	#   - Bystanders are forbidden from final stamp footprints, so preserved pixels do
    75	#     not get entangled with merged templates.
    76	#
    77	# g) Color-change inferability
    78	#   - The only semantic color split is structural and locally shown in the legend:
    79	#     teal cells are the mutable positions, non-teal cells are fixed positions.
    80	#   - No priority depends on hidden color ids. The visible priority is role-based:
    81	#     fixed template color beats recolored teal whenever both claim the same cell.
    82	#
    83	# main() assumption audit after the combo
    84	#   - _extract_template assumes every non-background cell inside the teal
    85	#     component's Chebyshev-1 halo belongs to that template. Generator enforces
    86	#     this via legend halos.
    87	#   - _resolve_bucket assumes same-layer overlaps never need a meaningful tie-break
    88	#     between different colors. Generator enforces this by rejecting such cases.
    89	#   - The source-color-to-rule mapping is still unique across rules. Generator
    90	#     preserves the original pair selection regime.
    91	#   - Preserved bystanders are assumed to lie outside all translated template
    92	#     footprints. Generator enforces that directly.
